(vector (map (:adr/id 2791003359), (:adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-3359 — Government Regulatory Associate Professionals Not Elsewhere Classified Documentation and Logistics-Coordination Coverage"), (:adr/status :accepted), (:adr/date "2026-07-17"), (:adr/doc_type "adr"), (:adr/source-format "edn"), (:adr/scope (vector :kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3359)), (:adr/references (vector 2607121000 2790003353)), (:actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-3359), (:actor/name "Regulatory Filing Coordination Practice"), (:actor/domain :government/regulatory-filing-coordination), (:actor/isco-08 "3359"), (:actor/wave 2), (:actor/robotics? true), (:actor/coordination? true), (:actor/proposal-ops (vector :log-inspection-record :schedule-review-appointment :flag-compliance-concern :coordinate-supply-order)), (:actor/always-escalate-ops (vector :flag-compliance-concern)), (:actor/hard-checks (vector :no-office :no-actuation :disallowed-op :unknown-case :case-wrong-office :scope-exclusion-hit)), (:actor/scope-excluded-terms (vector "issue the compliance ruling" "issue a compliance ruling" "impose the regulatory penalty" "impose a regulatory penalty" "order the enforcement action" "order an enforcement action")), (:operation/nodes (vector :intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold)), (:operation/entry-point :intake), (:registry/maturity :implemented), (:registry/required-technologies (vector :robotics :identity :audit-ledger)), (:registry/operating-states (vector :intake :propose :approve :execute :audit)), (:registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3359"), (:registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-3359"), (:test/counts "{:total 19, :assertions 45, :failures 0, :errors 0}"), (:test/coverage (vector :governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :office-verification :case-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation)), (:test/simulator :regfiling.actor-test), (:db/id -1), (:adr/body "# ADR 2791003359: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 3359 Government Regulatory Associate Professionals Not Elsewhere Classified documentation/logistics-coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 3359 -- Government\nRegulatory Associate Professionals Not Elsewhere Classified** only.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"3359\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"3359\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present), and its `:name` read exactly \"Government\nRegulatory Associate Professionals Not Elsewhere Classified\" (not\ntruncated) -- confirming the premise. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with no `:repo`/`:business-id`, and its\n`:required-technologies` were `[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]`; no\nrepository existed at `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3359` (`gh api`\n404-confirmed before scaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry\nedit promote the `3359` entry to `:implemented` without altering its\n`:required-technologies`.\n\n**This occupation class carries generic government regulatory\nenforcement authority in the real world.** ISCO-08 3359 is a catch-all\nfor government regulatory officials not covered by the more specific\n335x classes (customs 3351, tax/excise 3352, social benefits 3353,\nlicensing 3354, police inspectors/detectives 3355), and it broadly\ncovers officials who enforce regulations, issue compliance rulings and\nimpose penalties across various government domains. This is exactly\nthe class of risk `cloud-itonami-isco-3353` (Government Social Benefits\nOfficials, ADR-2790003353) already established a structural-exclusion\npattern for -- a decision domain severe enough that the *capability*\nitself must not exist in code, not merely be gated behind a confidence\nthreshold or human-approval interrupt. This ADR applies that same\nstructural-exclusion pattern to a general regulatory-enforcement\ncontext: no op resembling issuing a compliance ruling, imposing a\nregulatory penalty, or ordering an enforcement action exists anywhere\nin this actor.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3313)\n(Accounting Associate Professionals, independently re-cloned and\nre-read in full this session) -- `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`\n`.cljc` modules on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), plus\nthe full doc set (`blueprint.edn`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, `GOVERNANCE.md`, `LICENSE` AGPL-3.0-or-later,\n`README.md`, `SECURITY.md`, `docs/business-model.md`,\n`docs/operator-guide.md`). Domain substance was adapted substantially\naway from 3313's client/account/ceiling shape and toward\n`cloud-itonami-isco-3353`'s closed-allowlist + content-based\nscope-exclusion shape, per the enforcement-authority guidance above.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3359` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for government regulatory\nassociate professionals not elsewhere classified, following the SAME\ngoverned-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`\nactor in this fleet: **Regulatory Filing Advisor ⊣ RegFiling\nGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, with an independent\nGovernor, human-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an\nappend-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **documentation and logistics coordination\nONLY, never regulatory-enforcement or compliance-ruling authority** --\nit must NEVER be designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose\nexercising ANY regulatory-enforcement or compliance-ruling authority:\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-inspection-record` -- inspection/filing-status data entry\n  (documentation only, not a ruling)\n- `:schedule-review-appointment` -- compliance-review appointment\n  scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- surface an observation that MAY\n  warrant human regulatory-official review (discrepancy,\n  non-compliance pattern, risk indicator), ALWAYS escalates\n  immediately, NEVER auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- office/inspection-equipment\n  procurement proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles**:\nissuing a compliance ruling, imposing a regulatory penalty, or\nordering an enforcement action. These are **structurally absent from\nthe op-allowlist entirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an\nop such as `:issue-compliance-ruling` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer` function only ever emits one of\nthe four allowlist ops, falling back to an explicitly-labeled\n\"unrecognized op\" proposal for anything else), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:disallowed-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently --\nverified end to end through the compiled actor graph, not just at the\ngovernor's pure-function layer\n(`hard-on-disallowed-op-issue-compliance-ruling`/\n`-impose-regulatory-penalty`/`-order-enforcement-action` in\n`regfiling.governor-test`, and `holds-a-disallowed-op-never-commits` in\n`regfiling.actor-test`).\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Office unregistered** (`:no-office`) -- any proposal must resolve\n   to an independently registered government office/regulatory unit.\n   Never trusts the proposal's own claim.\n2. **Effect not `:propose`** (`:no-actuation`) -- any other `:effect`\n   value is a HARD block (a claim to directly actuate outside\n   governance).\n3. **Op not allowed** (`:disallowed-op`) -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block. This is the\n   central structural guarantee described above.\n4. **Case basis** (`:unknown-case` / `:case-wrong-office`) -- any\n   proposal referencing a `:case-id` (inspection log, review\n   appointment, compliance flag) must resolve to a REGISTERED case\n   belonging to the request's registered office.\n5. **Scope exclusion** (`:scope-exclusion-hit`) -- independent of the\n   op-allowlist check, any proposal whose free text (`:rationale`,\n   `:record-detail`, `:concern-detail`) names a finalization/execution\n   action for issuing a compliance ruling, imposing a regulatory\n   penalty, or ordering an enforcement action (e.g. \"issue the\n   compliance ruling\", \"impose the regulatory penalty\") is a HARD,\n   PERMANENT block, evaluated unconditionally on content, not just on\n   the `:op` field. This is defense-in-depth on top of #3: even if a\n   future change somehow widened the allowlist, content-based\n   rejection still independently blocks enforcement-authority\n   language.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the\ngeneral confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-compliance-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human regulatory\n  official, regardless of confidence. This is the ONLY channel by\n  which the robot may surface an observation suggesting a compliance\n  ruling, penalty, or enforcement action might be warranted -- it\n  never resolves the observation itself, and there is no auto-commit\n  path for it.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds the\n  office's registered `:max-supply-order-cost` -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. Unlike `cloud-itonami-isco-\n  3313`'s per-account transaction-amount ceiling (a HARD block), this\n  ceiling is deliberately an ESCALATE-only signal: this actor never\n  blocks procurement outright, it always lets a human decide\n  above-threshold spend. The boundary is inclusive (verified by\n  `ok-at-exact-threshold-boundary`).\n- (confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the\nsibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"ruling\", \"penalty\") can accidentally match inside the mock advisor's\nown default rationale text for a legitimate proposal, self-blocking\nthe happy path -- this is exactly the risk this vertical's own\nvocabulary could trigger, since a legitimate `:flag-compliance-concern`\nproposal must be able to say a compliance ruling or penalty *might\neventually be warranted* without that mention itself being treated as\nfinalizing one. `regfiling.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases` is\nphrased exclusively as finalization/execution ACTION phrases (e.g.\n\"issue the compliance ruling\", \"impose the regulatory penalty\", \"order\nthe enforcement action\"), never bare nouns, and\n`regfiling.advisor`'s mock rationale for `:flag-compliance-concern`\ndeliberately DOES mention the bare nouns \"ruling\" and \"penalty\" as a\nbenign observation, specifically to exercise this guard.\n`regfiling.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-exclusion-hit` (or any other HARD violation). Green on first\nrun in this session after one unrelated compile-time fix (a missing\n`office-id` destructure in the advisor's `:coordinate-supply-order`\nbranch) -- the self-trip bug itself was not hit, but this ADR\ndocuments the guard and its regression test per the known fleet risk\nclass.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"3359\" ...}` entry is\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-3359` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3359`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed from the live registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not by `grep -c`, immediately\n  before the edit to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch). `:required-technologies` is left unchanged\n  (`[:robotics :identity :audit-ledger]`) -- this ADR's registry edit\n  is scoped to the maturity/repo/business-id fields only.\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 19 tests, 45 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just the local\n  working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- consistent with the live convention already confirmed\n  by sibling ISCO actors this batch; only the central\n  `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry itself depends on it, to resolve\n  `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with a `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- This ADR's structural-exclusion design (Decision section) follows\n  the reference pattern set by ADR-2790003353 (closed allowlist +\n  content-based scope exclusion + single always-escalate flag-op),\n  applied here to a general regulatory-enforcement-authority context\n  rather than a benefits-eligibility context -- both are instances of\n  the same underlying rule: when the real-world decision is severe\n  enough (harming a person's subsistence, or exercising government\n  enforcement power over a person/business), the *capability* itself\n  must not exist in code, not merely be deferred to a human gate.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an `:issue-compliance-ruling`/`:impose-regulatory-\n  penalty` op that is merely gated behind `always-escalate-ops` (the\n  same shape `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for\n  `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`): rejected outright per the explicit\n  task guidance and the ADR-2790003353 precedent -- an always-escalate\n  op still means the *capability* to issue a ruling or impose a\n  penalty exists in the code path and is merely deferred to a human\n  gate; for authority this severe, the capability itself must not\n  exist. The only op related to potential enforcement is\n  `:flag-compliance-concern`, which surfaces the need for a decision\n  to a human and never itself decides, rules, or defers a ruling/\n  penalty action for later automatic execution.\n- A numeric ceiling/ ranking check mirroring 3313's per-account\n  transaction-amount ceiling, applied to some notion of \"penalty\n  severity\": rejected -- there is no `:op` that imposes a penalty at\n  all, so no severity ceiling is meaningful; the only numeric ceiling\n  in this actor is the unrelated, genuinely low-stakes\n  `:coordinate-supply-order` cost threshold (office-equipment\n  procurement), where an amount ceiling is an appropriate,\n  proportionate ESCALATE-only control (deliberately not a HARD block,\n  since procurement itself carries no enforcement risk).\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"ruling\" \"penalty\"\n  \"enforcement\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip\n  bug class described above -- this actor's own domain necessarily\n  discusses ruling/penalty concepts to do its documentation job (e.g.\n  `:flag-compliance-concern`'s purpose IS to flag that a ruling or\n  penalty might eventually be warranted), so a bare-noun list would\n  have self-blocked the actor's own legitimate proposals. Verified by\n  a dedicated regression test rather than left as an untested\n  assumption.\n- Treating `case` as an unverified free-text field instead of a\n  provenance-checked entity (mirroring only `office` verification):\n  rejected -- documentation proposals against an unverified/foreign\n  case would let a proposal route logging, scheduling or escalation\n  through an unregistered or wrong-office case record, so `case` gets\n  the same independent registered+office-matched HARD check as\n  `office`, matching the two-entity provenance shape already\n  established by prior sibling actors' client/account and applicant/\n  office patterns. (The protocol method is named `filing-case`, not\n  `case`, in `regfiling.store` to avoid shadowing the\n  `clojure.core/case` macro within the same namespace.)")))
